
export anchor GiveCardsTrelloSDragAndLinearSMotion2:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        On Planning's board, dragging a card feels like Trello. Only the board does this; spec/ui/behaviours/drag-reorder/index.pi#DragReorderBehaviour stays as it is everywhere else, apart from a line saying a user may open the gap. The card is picked up as a full-size copy, tilted a few degrees, on the overlay surface with the shadow spec/ui/theme/index.pi#Theme.elevation allows for floating things. Where it would land, the cards slide apart to open a placeholder the card's height, in place of the accent line alone. On drop, the card settles into that gap. Cards moved any other way animate to their new place with quick motion of about 150 ms, in Linear's style. That covers the card menu, Alt+arrows, Ctrl+Enter, Send sprint, and Close sprint, as spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.sprint describes those moves. The side panel slides in from the right and out again. Nothing animates while the user is waiting on it: the target gap still updates at once as the pointer moves. All of this is described in spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning first, then built.
        \\\

    mode: code

    newConversation: true

    systemPrompt:
        \\\
        We're working on the code, located in ./src. Change the code so it does what the prompt asks, as the spec describes it.

        When the work is about Suspense, its modes, its prompts, or its questions, or you are to write a prompt or a question for the user, first read .suspense/suspense-fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Suspense works.

        The spec's Piton source is in ./spec. Don't change it, and don't read it to learn what the spec says: read the compiled reference instead, as above. If the prompt asks for something the spec doesn't describe, or describes differently, say so in your reply rather than working around it.

        Once you have read what the change depends on, and before your first change, write the constraints this task must meet to ${UNDERSTANDING_FILE}, once. Rewrite it only when a constraint in it turns out to be wrong, never after each page you read. It is a Markdown list and nothing else: one line per constraint, at most twelve, each a single short, specific statement of what must be true, written as a link to the compiled reference file it comes from, such as `- [The chain tab has no bottom border in combined mode](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/chat-input/index.md#chain)`. Only list what the spec says; leave out anything you are deciding yourself. Replace a constraint when you learn it was wrong rather than adding another.

        End with a reply of a few sentences saying what changed, or for a question, the answer, not a recap of the work or of what you read.

        This prompt is the second step of a chain. Its first step changed the spec to meet it, and the spec has been built since. Now change the code so it does what the spec now says, without changing the spec. Follow the spec the prompt references, and the anchors the spec step names below. Where the spec and the prompt disagree, follow the spec, and say so in your reply.

        The code is located in ./src.

        The spec's source, which this step doesn't change, is in ./spec.

        The prompt the chain was sent:

        ${SPEC_PROMPT}

        What the spec step said it changed, its final output:

        ${SPEC_RESULT}
        \\\

    codeTask:
        prompt:
            \\\
            On Planning's board, dragging a card feels like Trello. Only the board does this; spec/ui/behaviours/drag-reorder/index.pi#DragReorderBehaviour stays as it is everywhere else, apart from a line saying a user may open the gap. The card is picked up as a full-size copy, tilted a few degrees, on the overlay surface with the shadow spec/ui/theme/index.pi#Theme.elevation allows for floating things. Where it would land, the cards slide apart to open a placeholder the card's height, in place of the accent line alone. On drop, the card settles into that gap. Cards moved any other way animate to their new place with quick motion of about 150 ms, in Linear's style. That covers the card menu, Alt+arrows, Ctrl+Enter, Send sprint, and Close sprint, as spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.sprint describes those moves. The side panel slides in from the right and out again. Nothing animates while the user is waiting on it: the target gap still updates at once as the pointer moves. All of this is described in spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning first, then built.
            \\\
        result:
            \\\
            The spec now describes the Trello-style drag and the Linear-style motion. `piton check` reports no problems and `piton build` succeeded.

            **What changed**
            - **New `Planning.motion` section** (`spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.motion`):
              - **Picking up:** the card is picked up as a full-size copy tilted about 3°, on the overlay surface with its edge border and the shadow `Theme.elevation` allows for floating things. Its old place stays open, the card's height, until the pointer leaves the column.
              - **The gap:** where the card would land, the cards slide apart to open one placeholder the card's height, 8 px from its neighbours, on the well surface with 6 px rounded corners. Columns that take no drops open no gap.
              - **Nothing waits on animation:** which gap is chosen still updates at once as the pointer moves, and a drop lands there even while the cards are still sliding.
              - **Dropping:** the copy settles into the gap. If it's dropped anywhere else, or Escape is pressed, it settles back where it came from.
              - **Other moves:** they slide to their new place in about 150 ms, eased out. That covers the card menu, Alt+arrows, Ctrl+Enter, Send sprint and Close sprint, plus cards being added or deleted. Focus and saving happen at once, and a new move starts from wherever the card is mid-motion.
              - **Side panel:** it slides in from the right and back out. Swapping what it shows doesn't slide.
            - **`Planning.sprint`:** the drag sentence now says the gap opens "as motion says", and Current sprint and Done "open no gap" instead of showing no insertion line.
            - **`Planning.layout`:** one line saying the side panel slides in and out as motion says.
            - **`DragReorderBehaviour.indicator`:** one paragraph saying a user may open the gap instead, in which case that user's own description applies. Everything else in the behaviour is unchanged, so step reordering in an open story keeps the accent line.

            **Anchors the code should follow:** `spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.motion` (the main one), `#Planning.sprint`, `#Planning.layout` and `spec/ui/behaviours/drag-reorder/index.pi#DragReorderBehaviour.indicator`.
            \\\

    sentFrom: GiveCardsTrelloSDragAndLinearSMotion

    model: opus
